1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,202
Getting a tax refund this year?

2
00:00:02,622 --> 00:00:06,347
Decide where it goes now, before it lands and quietly disappears.

3
00:00:06,767 --> 00:00:10,410
Our example: a $2,000 refund, arriving in March.

4
00:00:11,180 --> 00:00:14,263
Here's a simple decision path, one question at a time.

5
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:21,413
First, take 10% for something fun: $200. You'll stick to a plan better when part of it is yours.

6
00:00:22,183 --> 00:00:25,289
Then ask four questions, in order. Stop at the first no.

7
00:00:25,709 --> 00:00:26,440
Here they are.

8
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:33,368
One. Are all your bills caught up? If not, the refund catches them up. Late fees and shutoffs cost more than anything.

9
00:00:33,788 --> 00:00:38,540
Two. Do you have a starter emergency fund, one month of bills? If not, start there.

10
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,495
Three. Is your high-interest debt gone? If not, that's where it goes.

11
00:00:42,915 --> 00:00:46,126
Four. Are your sinking funds on track? If not, top them up.

12
00:00:46,546 --> 00:00:49,263
All yes? Then it goes to your bigger savings goals.

13
00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:52,871
Here's why question three beats a savings account.

14
00:00:53,291 --> 00:01:03,164
$1,800 in savings at 4% earns about $72 in a year. Put on a 24% card, it saves about $432 in interest.

15
00:01:03,584 --> 00:01:13,603
On the $10,000 card from episode 11, paying $500 a month, that $1,800 finishes it 5 months sooner and saves about $905 in interest.

16
00:01:14,373 --> 00:01:15,214
One more thing.

17
00:01:15,634 --> 00:01:24,642
A big refund means you overpaid tax all year. In the US, you can adjust your W-4 withholding, so that money comes in each paycheck instead.

18
00:01:25,062 --> 00:01:30,321
$2,000 spread over 26 paychecks is about $77 more per check.

19
00:01:30,741 --> 00:01:38,110
Some people like the forced savings of a refund, and that's fine. Just make it a choice. If you're unsure, ask a tax professional.

20
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:43,822
The Paycheck Planner in the description shows where every dollar is going, refund included.

21
00:01:44,242 --> 00:01:47,556
Subscribe. Next time: how much rent you can really afford.
